        text: `In Tern Harbour, you paid for everything with something you could spare, and nobody agreed on what that meant. Ilya stood at the weigh-stall with her hands behind her back, because the stallholder always watched a customer's hands first.

"One bottle of fog-oil," she said. "For the lamp on my grandmother's boat."

The stallholder, a woman with a brass scale where one eye should have been, tilted her head. "Boats don't need lamps in the dry months. The fog doesn't come until the third tide-bell."

"It's coming early," Ilya said.

"Is it." The scale-eye clicked and whirred. Behind Ilya, a line of fishers had gone quiet. Everyone in Tern knew the rule: say a thing is coming, and the harbour might decide to prove you right, then send you the bill.

Ilya had broken the rule on purpose. She opened her hand and set a small glass jar on the counter. Inside, a single grey feather turned slowly in nothing, the way things do when they remember wind.

"That's a lullaby-feather," the woman said, softer now. "Where did a child get a lullaby-feather?"

"It was my mother's."

"Then it's worth more than the oil. Much more." The woman did not touch the jar. "Which means I can't take it without taking something else along with it. Your mother's name, perhaps. Or the song."

Ilya had rehearsed this part all the way down the hill, and still her voice came out small. "Just the oil. Please. I'll owe you the difference."

"Nobody owes in Tern," said the woman. "We settle, or we leave."

Out beyond the harbour wall, the first bell tolled, far too early, and the water began to go white.`
